# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
A(('h1', S1))
A(('p', 'By the end of this study, you should be able to:'))
A(('bul', [
    'Follow the parable of the net in the order Jesus tells it: a net cast into the sea, a net that is full, a drawing to shore, a sitting down and a sorting.',
    'Separate what Jesus says in His own explanation of the net (13:49–50) from the parts of the story He leaves unnamed, and record His words exactly.',
    'Read the closing saying about the scribe and the householder (13:52) as a second comparison that Jesus gives and does not explain.',
    'State what the two comparisons say without assigning an identity to the sea, the net, the fishermen, the vessels, the scribe, the householder or the treasure.',
    'Keep this parable apart from the other parables of Matthew 13 and refuse to carry the meaning of any other passage into it.',
    'Place the parable in Jesus’ earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, hold the boundary where the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins, and state a principled lesson for today.',
]))
A(('call', 'Study Goal',
   'To read Matthew 13:47–52 exactly as Jesus gave it, to record what He explains and what He leaves unexplained, and to state what the net and the scribe comparisons say without adding an identity, a symbol or a doctrine the text does not supply.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. The parables of Matthew 13 are told on one day. “The same day went Jesus out of the house, and sat by the sea side” (13:1), and “great multitudes” gathered to Him (13:2). To the multitude He spoke in parables: “All these things spake Jesus unto the multitude in parables; and without a parable spake he not unto them” (13:34). Then “Jesus sent the multitude away, and went into the house: and his disciples came unto him” (13:36), and they asked Him to declare an earlier parable of that day.'))
A(('p', '2.2 Where this parable stands. The net comes after that move into the house. Matthew does not say that the multitude came back or that the disciples were sent away. When the parables end, the text reads: “And it came to pass, that when Jesus had finished these parables, he departed thence” (13:53). The net is therefore among the last words of the day, and it is followed at once by a question that Jesus puts to those who heard it: “Have ye understood all these things? They say unto him, Yea, Lord” (13:51).'))
A(('call', 'No Question Introduces This Parable',
   'No question or remark from a hearer comes before the net. It begins with the opening Jesus has used through the chapter, “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a net” (13:47). The question Matthew records near it is Jesus’ own, “Have ye understood all these things?” (13:51), and it comes after the parable and its explanation.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The setting before the scene. Matthew 12 shows the opposition that stands behind the day of the parables. The Pharisees “held a council against him, how they might destroy him” (12:14), and they said, “This fellow doth not cast out devils, but by Beelzebub the prince of the devils” (12:24). The net is told after that refusal had been spoken.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Matthew has said what Jesus preached: “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (4:17). He sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. And as ye go, preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand” (10:5–7). The net and the saying about the scribe use the same words, “the kingdom of heaven,” and they are spoken to people who have heard that the Kingdom is at hand.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Matthew gives two reasons for parables in this chapter. Jesus says, “Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given” (13:11), and Matthew says that the parables fulfilled the words, “I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things which have been kept secret from the foundation of the world” (13:35). The net is part of what is uttered on that day. It is not a free-floating saying about the world and it is not a rule for every person in every age.'))
A(('ctbl', 'The verses that set the scene are laid out below.', [
    ['Verse', 'KJV text', 'What to observe'],
    ['Matt 13:1–2', '“The same day went Jesus out of the house”; “great multitudes”', 'One day of many parables.'],
    ['Matt 13:36', '“Then Jesus sent the multitude away, and went into the house”', 'The move indoors, to the disciples.'],
    ['Matt 13:47', '“Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a net”', 'The comparison, joined by “Again.”'],
    ['Matt 13:51', '“Have ye understood all these things?”', 'The question Jesus puts, and the answer given.'],
    ['Matt 13:53', '“when Jesus had finished these parables”', 'The close of the day of parables.'],
    ['Matt 4:17; 10:5–7', '“the kingdom of heaven is at hand”', 'What was preached, and to whom.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The passage is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The net (13:47–48). “Again, the kingdom of heaven is like unto a net, that was cast into the sea, and gathered of every kind: Which, when it was full, they drew to shore, and sat down, and gathered the good into vessels, but cast the bad away.” The sequence has six steps. A net is cast into the sea. It gathers “of every kind.” It becomes full. “They” draw it to shore. They sit down. They gather the good into vessels and cast the bad away.'))
A(('p', '3.2 What the story says and does not say. The text says the net “was cast” and does not say who cast it. It says the net “gathered of every kind,” and it says that “when it was full” the drawing began. It says “they” drew it to shore and sat down, and does not say who “they” are. It says the sorting is “the good” into vessels and “the bad” cast away. It does not say how the good are told from the bad, what the vessels are, or what becomes of the bad that are cast away. It does not mention a field, a sower, an enemy, a harvest or a delay.'))
A(('p', '3.3 What follows the story (13:49–50). Jesus does not end the parable at the casting away. The next two verses begin with “So shall it be,” and they are Jesus’ own words about the story. They are set out word for word in Section 4, where Jesus’ identifications are recorded, and they are not paraphrased here.'))
A(('p', '3.4 The question and the answer (13:51). “Jesus saith unto them, Have ye understood all these things? They say unto him, Yea, Lord.” Jesus asks; the disciples answer. The text records no further word from them.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The scribe and the householder (13:52). “Then said he unto them, Therefore every scribe which is instructed unto the kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that is an householder, which bringeth forth out of his treasure things new and old.” The sequence has four parts. It begins with “Therefore,” so the saying is drawn from what has just been said. A scribe is named, and he is “instructed unto the kingdom of heaven.” That scribe is “like unto a man that is an householder.” The householder brings forth out of his treasure “things new and old.”'))
A(('p', '3.6 What the saying says and does not say. The text says the instructed scribe is like a householder, and it says what the householder does: he brings forth. It does not say what the treasure is, what the things called “new and old” are. It does not say who the scribe is beyond “instructed unto the kingdom of heaven.” It gives no story and no outcome for this man, and no explanation follows it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:47', '“a net, that was cast into the sea, and gathered of every kind”', 'A net is cast and gathers of every kind.'],
    ['Matt 13:48', '“when it was full, they drew to shore, and sat down”', 'The drawing and the sitting down come after the net is full.'],
    ['Matt 13:48', '“gathered the good into vessels, but cast the bad away”', 'The sorting is the last act of the story.'],
    ['Matt 13:49–50', '“So shall it be at the end of the world”', 'Jesus’ own words follow the story; see Section 4.'],
    ['Matt 13:51', '“Have ye understood all these things?”', 'The question, with the disciples’ answer.'],
    ['Matt 13:52', '“every scribe which is instructed unto the kingdom of heaven”', 'A second comparison, introduced by “Therefore.”'],
    ['Matt 13:52', '“bringeth forth out of his treasure things new and old”', 'What the householder does.'],
], [0.17, 0.43, 0.40]))
A(('p', '3.7 What the passage leaves unsaid. In the story Jesus does not say who casts the net, who “they” are, what the sea or the vessels are, or what “every kind” includes. In the saying He does not say what the treasure or the things new and old are. The parable gives a net that gathers, a full net, a drawing, a sitting down and a sorting. The saying gives a scribe, a householder, a treasure and things brought forth.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus names. Jesus explains the net. After the story He says: “So shall it be at the end of the world: the angels shall come forth, and sever the wicked from among the just, And shall cast them into the furnace of fire: there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth” (13:49–50). Everything Jesus identifies is in those two verses, and the table records it in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the story', 'What Jesus says', 'Verse'],
    ['The time of the sorting', '“at the end of the world”', 'Matt 13:49'],
    ['Who comes forth', '“the angels shall come forth”', 'Matt 13:49'],
    ['The sorting', '“sever the wicked from among the just”', 'Matt 13:49'],
    ['The fate of those severed', '“cast them into the furnace of fire”', 'Matt 13:50'],
    ['What follows', '“there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth”', 'Matt 13:50'],
], [0.28, 0.54, 0.18]))
A(('p', '4.2 What Jesus says will happen. Jesus says, “So shall it be at the end of the world.” He then names those who act, the angels, and those acted upon, the wicked, who are severed “from among the just.” He says they are cast “into the furnace of fire,” and that “there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth.” These are the whole of His explanation, and none of it is added to here.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name in the story. The explanation names the angels, the wicked and the just, the furnace, and the end of the world. It leaves the other parts of the story without an identification. Jesus does not identify the net. Jesus does not identify the sea. Jesus does not identify the “every kind” that the net gathers. Jesus does not identify the ones who cast the net. Jesus does not identify “they” who drew it to shore and sat down. Jesus does not identify the shore. Jesus does not identify the vessels. Jesus does not state that “the good” and “the bad” of 13:48 are the “just” and the “wicked” of 13:49 in so many words; He says “So shall it be,” and then names the wicked and the just. Jesus does not state that the angels are the ones who drew the net. Jesus also does not say what becomes of the good that are gathered into vessels, and the explanation speaks only of those who are severed and cast.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only the end of the world, the angels, the wicked, the just, the furnace of fire and the wailing and gnashing of teeth, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the net is a certain body, that the sea is a certain people, that the fishermen are certain persons or that the vessels are a certain place is supplying what the Lord did not. The field and the world of 13:38 belong to the explanation of that earlier parable and are not carried into this one.'))
A(('p', '4.4 The saying about the scribe has no explanation. After the net Jesus asks, “Have ye understood all these things?” and the disciples say, “Yea, Lord” (13:51). He then gives the saying of 13:52 and gives no explanation of it. This section therefore records no identification for it. Jesus does not identify the scribe beyond “instructed unto the kingdom of heaven.” Jesus does not identify the householder; the householder is the thing the scribe is compared to. Jesus does not identify the treasure. Jesus does not identify the things He calls “new and old.” Jesus does not identify what “bringeth forth” is.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['Part of the saying', 'What Jesus says it is', 'Verse'],
    ['The scribe', '“instructed unto the kingdom of heaven”; nothing further', 'Matt 13:52'],
    ['The householder', 'Jesus does not identify him.', 'Matt 13:52'],
    ['The treasure', 'Jesus does not identify it.', 'Matt 13:52'],
    ['Things “new and old”', 'Jesus does not identify them.', 'Matt 13:52'],
], [0.28, 0.54, 0.18]))
A(('p', '4.5 The explanation stays with this parable. The words of 13:49–50 explain the net and are not carried to any other parable. The end of the world, the angels and the furnace of fire appear in His explanation of an earlier parable that same day (13:39–42), and Jesus says the same words there for that story. The shared words do not make the two stories one, and neither explanation supplies the identifications the other leaves out.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparisons themselves. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story or of the saying.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison of the net. Jesus says the kingdom of heaven is like a net that gathered of every kind. The comparison is to the whole movement of the story: a gathering that takes in without sorting, a net that is allowed to fill, and a sorting that comes after the net is drawn to shore. The meaning of the comparison is in the order of those acts.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The gathering is wide.** The net gathers “of every kind.” The Kingdom is compared to something that takes in what comes, and the text does not say that the net chooses as it gathers.',
    '**The sorting is later.** The sorting begins only “when it was full” and the net is on the shore. What is gathered together is not sorted while the net is in the sea.',
    '**The sorting is complete.** The good are gathered into vessels and the bad are cast away. The story leaves nothing in between.',
    '**The sorting is not the net’s work.** The net gathers; “they” sit down and sort. The comparison places the sorting in other hands.',
    '**The time of the sorting is given by Jesus, not by the comparison.** The comparison shows that a sorting comes; the time and those who act are stated by Jesus in Section 4, and this section adds none.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the comparison of the scribe communicates. The saying compares an instructed scribe to a householder who brings forth out of his treasure things new and old. The comparison shows a man with a store, and it shows him drawing on it. It shows that the store holds more than one kind of thing, “new and old,” and that he brings both out.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The instruction comes first.** The scribe is “instructed unto the kingdom of heaven” before he brings anything out.',
    '**The store holds more than one thing.** The treasure yields things “new and old.” The comparison does not say which is which.',
    '**The use of the store is active.** The householder “bringeth forth.” He does not leave the store closed.',
]))
A(('p', '5.4 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers had heard that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17; 10:7). They were meant to understand, from the net, that the Kingdom as Jesus described it includes a wide gathering that is followed by a sorting that is certain, and, from the saying, that one who is taught the Kingdom has a store to draw from. These are comparisons about the Kingdom, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('call', 'What This Section Does Not Do',
   'It does not say what the net, the sea or the vessels are. It does not say who the fishermen are. It does not say what the treasure or the things new and old are, and it does not say that the scribe is any particular person. It does not turn gathering, drawing, sorting or bringing forth into steps by which a person is saved. It draws no meaning from another parable into these two.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The net and the saying about the scribe belong to the last of the parables of Matthew 13. They are kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:24–30, 36–43', '“sowed good seed in his field”; “The field is the world”; “gather out of his kingdom all things that offend”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. A field, a sower, an enemy, servants, a delay and reapers. Jesus names the field there. That naming is for that parable only; the sea and the net of 13:47 are not the field of 13:38, and there is no enemy or delay in the net.'],
    ['Matt 13:44–46', '“treasure hid in a field”; “a merchant man, seeking goodly pearls”', 'The Hidden Treasure and the Pearl. A finding, a selling and a buying, with no explanation. The “treasure” of 13:44 stands in a story about a man who finds; the “treasure” of 13:52 stands in a saying about a householder. They are not the same treasure.'],
    ['Matt 13:31–33', '“a grain of mustard seed”; “leaven”', 'The Mustard Seed and the Leaven. Things that grow or work through, with no sorting. No net, no shore and no vessels.'],
], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The net is told after the move into the house (13:36) and closes the day (13:53). The Wheat and the Weeds is told to the multitude and explained in the house (13:34, 36). The wording “at the end of the world” and “wailing and gnashing of teeth” occurs in Jesus’ explanation of the earlier parable (13:39, 42) and in His explanation of the net (13:49–50). Other passages in Matthew use similar words of other persons in other settings (8:12; 22:13; 24:51; 25:30), and each of them says “weeping and gnashing of teeth,” not “wailing.” Each is read in its own setting.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The net story has an unnamed company that casts, draws and sorts, together with the angels of Jesus’ explanation. The Wheat and the Weeds has a sower, an enemy, servants and reapers. The treasure and the pearl have a man who finds and a merchant who seeks. Matthew 13:52 has a scribe and a householder. No character is carried from one to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The net moves from casting to filling to drawing to sorting. The Wheat and the Weeds moves from sowing to a delayed harvest. The treasure and the pearl move from finding to selling to buying. The Mustard Seed and the Leaven move from small to large. None of those movements is read into the net.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the “sea” and the “net” of 13:47 are not “the field” and “the world” of 13:38. Second, the “good” and the “bad” gathered into vessels and cast away in 13:48 are not the “good seed” and the “tares” of 13:38, and the story has no seed. Third, the “treasure” of 13:52 is not the hid treasure of 13:44 and is not named by Jesus.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). The net and the saying about the scribe belong to that ministry. The Kingdom they speak of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and the text places them among the things the disciples are asked about at the end of the day (13:51).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mysteries of the kingdom of heaven” that Jesus says are given to the disciples (13:11) are His own Kingdom matters given to them during His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The net, the sea, the vessels and the furnace are not a description of the Body of Christ, and the sorting is not its salvation or its judgment. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God or of what any person faces after death. The gathering, the drawing and the sorting are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The scribe of 13:52 is likewise a figure in Jesus’ Kingdom teaching, and the saying is not a charter for the office of teaching in the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparisons show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of restraint.** In the story the sorting comes after the net is full and on the shore, and it is not done while the net is in the sea. A reader learns not to take up a sorting that the story places later and in other hands.',
    '**A lesson of seriousness.** The story ends in a sorting that is complete. A reader learns to treat the worth of the Kingdom, and the gravity of what Jesus says of its end, with weight.',
    '**A lesson of understanding.** Jesus asks, “Have ye understood all these things?” A reader learns that hearing is to be followed by understanding, and that understanding is to be tested.',
    '**A lesson of use.** The householder brings forth out of his treasure. A reader learns that what has been taught is to be drawn on, not stored, and that a full store is used for more than one kind of need.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The net is not turned into the Body’s gathering, and the sorting is not turned into a warning laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its hope and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in these words of Jesus.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus compares the kingdom of heaven to a net cast into the sea that gathered of every kind, was drawn to shore when full, and was sorted, the good into vessels and the bad cast away. He explains the net in His own words: at the end of the world the angels shall sever the wicked from among the just and cast them into the furnace of fire, where there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth. He names nothing else in the story. He then compares the scribe instructed unto the kingdom of heaven to a householder who brings forth out of his treasure things new and old, and He gives no explanation of that saying. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says what the net, the sea, the vessels, the treasure or the things new and old are, or that makes the gathering and the sorting a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a net is cast, gathers of every kind, is full and is drawn to shore, and the good are gathered into vessels while the bad are cast away; a scribe instructed unto the kingdom of heaven is like a householder who brings forth things new and old.',
    '**The meaning:** the Kingdom is compared to a wide gathering followed by a complete sorting, and to a store from which one who is instructed draws.',
    '**The boundaries:** only the end of the world, the angels, the wicked, the just, the furnace and the wailing are named; the net, the sea, the vessels, the householder and the treasure are not; the field of 13:38 is not here.',
    '**The present lesson:** restraint, seriousness, understanding and use, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between what Jesus explains and what He leaves.** He explains the end of the world, the angels, the wicked and the just, the furnace and the wailing. He does not explain the net, the sea, the vessels or the ones who draw and sit. He gives no explanation of 13:52.',
    '**Between the story and the saying.** The net is a story with a sequence; the scribe is a one-sentence comparison. The saying begins with “Therefore” but gives no story and has no explanation.',
    '**Between characters.** The “they” who draw and sit are not named. The angels are named in the explanation and are not said to be the ones who draw. The scribe is not the householder; he is compared to him.',
    '**Between settings.** The net comes after the move into the house (13:36) and before the close of the parables (13:53).',
    '**Between this parable and its neighbors.** There is no field, no seed, no enemy and no harvest here. The field of 13:38 is not the sea of 13:47. The treasure of 13:52 is not the treasure of 13:44.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the net, the sea, the vessels, the fishermen, the householder, the treasure or the things new and old. The Body of Christ is neither the net nor the vessels.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the Wheat and the Weeds, the Hidden Treasure and the Pearl, or the Mustard Seed and the Leaven.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Casting, gathering, drawing, sitting, sorting and bringing forth are actions in a story and a saying. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

